Clipping ratio definition:
- In KVQuant and related work, the "clipping ratio" α refers to a percentile threshold on the magnitude distribution of the values to be quantized. Values above the α-th quantile of magnitude are clipped to the q-th quantile value before scaling/scaling-factor computation. E.g., a clipping ratio of α means you compute the scaling factor based on the α-quantile of the absolute values (e.g., 99.99th percentile) rather than the absmax. This is a percentile-based clipping definition.
- Alternatively, clipping ratio could be defined differently: some work uses a ratio r applied to absmax, i.e., scale = absmax * r, clip at scale. Need to clarify which definition.

The question says "symmetric input quantization" for the input (activations) and "asymmetric KV cache quantization" for the KV cache. KVQuant uses asymmetric quantization (with zero-point) for the KV cache because distributions are non-negative (keys and values are non-negative after certain processing — actually in KVQuant they observed asymmetric distributions). The input quantization is symmetric (around zero, no zero point). So:
- Input quantization: symmetric, so scale-based, with clipping ratio applied to the magnitude distribution percentile.
